-- Sharing metadata: read by tooling and the manager detail pane.
return {
  summary = "Hold B to walk faster -- and leave smoke, flames or lightning behind you, cut the tall grass you run through, or run past it without meeting a thing. Every extra has an off switch.",
  author = "MadeinTaly",
  support = "https://linktr.ee/made_in_taly",
  tags = { "quality-of-life", "movement", "beginner", "data-only" },
  compat = { modApi = 2, games = { "gen1", "gen2" } },
  differences = {
    added = {
      "holding B shortens a step, at a multiplier you choose: x1.5, x2, x3 or x4",
      "BOOST BIKE and BOOST SURF, both off by default",
      "RUN FX: a trail off your heels while you run -- off, smoke, flames or bolts -- reaching about three and a half cells back and fading the whole way out. Each particle is drawn as rows off a shape table: the puff is a disc, the flame a teardrop pinched at the tip and rounded at the base, the bolt thick at the ground and a hair at its tip",
      "RUN FX is shed a cell and a quarter BEHIND the heels and never draws inside the player's own 16x16 sprite box: it composites over the finished frame, so a mark in that box is a mark on the character rather than behind him",
      "BURN GRASS (off): tall grass you RUN across is really CUT, one clod at a time, following every cell of your path -- the tile stops being grass, so the engine stops rolling encounters on it by itself",
      "SAFE GRASS (off): running through tall grass never starts a wild battle",
    },
    changed = {
      "with the three new rows left alone, nothing but the duration of a step: a tile still costs a tile, and collision, triggers and the animation are untouched",
      "BURN GRASS writes the cut cells into mod.save, so entering the map again re-applies what was cut",
    },
    known = {
      "works inside the Dramatic Shape voxel mod's 1ST and 3RD cameras as of 1.7.0: those replace the grid step with a continuous camera-relative walk that never asks movement.speed, which used to leave every row of this mod silent until you went back overhead. RUN SPEED, BURN GRASS and SAFE GRASS all come back; RUN FX does not, because a screen-space overlay cannot follow a 3D camera",
      "the voxel mod is an OPTIONAL dependency, found through mod.find on a logic tick rather than at load, so neither mod needs the other and either can be enabled mid-session",
      "runs on Pokemon Gold, and every row works there: RUN SPEED, BOOST BIKE, BOOST SURF and SAFE GRASS all cross over unchanged, because Gold raises movement.speed with the same ctx keys and rebuilds the step duration every step",
      "BURN GRASS on Gold reads FieldMoves.CUT_BLOCKS, the table Gold's own CUT reads -- a per-tileset block swap with no single tile id to key off, so the one-clod cut there is a block DIFF instead of a tile match, and it splices a new collision entry alongside the new tile block because Gold's walkability is per-cell, not per-tile-id",
      "RUN FX on Gold uses the same Camera:follow projection Gen 1 does; the only Gen 2-specific part is knowing the overworld is really on screen, since Gold's overworld is not a stack state the way Gen 1's is",
      "the engine's own dust puff behind a running step has no Gen 2 backing, so on Gold the coloured particles carry the whole trail on their own",
      "x2 is exactly the bicycle's own 8 frames, so at that setting your shoes have caught up with a vehicle -- this is a fact about Gen 1's bicycle, not a bug in the shoes",
      "the ladder stops at x4 because steps are whole frames: one rung further is 3 frames, then 2, then 1, at which point a tile passes in a sixtieth of a second and the walk cycle is a strobe",
      "it calls the next handler first and multiplies the answer, so a mod that slows you down in a swamp keeps its say",
      "no sprint animation: the legs move at walking cadence, just over less ground-time -- Gen 1 has no running frames to draw",
      "scripted walks stay at the engine's own speed: a step's duration lives on the player and a cutscene's steps do not ask the hook, so until 1.1.0 an escort NPC and the player walked at different speeds and the player arrived ahead of the dialogue",
      "the coloured particles draw over the finished frame in true colour, so in the monochrome display modes they are the one thing on screen that is not a Game Boy shade; the puff underneath is the engine's own art and matches",
      "the coloured particles stand down while tilt mode or a world-replacing render pipeline owns the camera: a screen-space overlay cannot follow either. The puff and the cut are engine drawing and are unaffected",
      "SAFE GRASS still throws the vanilla dice and discards them, so the RNG stream stays where the engine left it -- only the battle is missing. BURN GRASS suppresses nothing at all: it cuts the tile before the encounter check looks at it",
    },
  },
}
